{
 "type": "instrument",
 "id": "harp",
 "name": "Concert harp",
 "version": 1,
 "note": "An instrument recipe for the sampler (doc 24). It describes exactly the files the prep step produced. Plucked, not looped: each file is trimmed to 3 ms before the onset (first point above -50 dB of the file's peak), resampled to 48 kHz, cut where its 50 ms RMS falls to -60 dB below peak or at 4 s, whichever comes first, and given a 250 ms equal-power fade to silence, as the piano game set. No gain change. Stereo as recorded. formats: <file>.ogg is Ogg Opus 80 kbps stereo (variable bitrate), <file>.m4a is AAC-LC 128 kbps at 48 kHz. Both decode with the onset in the same place (cross-correlation lag 0 samples, one file 1 sample); the .ogg decodes to exactly the WAV length and the .m4a about 0 to 21 ms longer (AAC end padding). Zone fields as in piano-steinway-b.json: measuredCents is the zone's pitch against 12-TET at A4 = 440 Hz, fitted from partials 1 to 6 of the louder layer (harp strings are close to harmonic; B is tiny); tuneConfidence 0 to 1. Layer fields: peakDb, attackRmsDb (first 300 ms), brightness (share of energy above max(1.5 f0, 1 kHz) in the first 300 ms), lenS, trimStartMs, measuredCents, endReason, src (original file name; the library is VCSL unless marked VSCO 2 CE). layers: v1 is mf (VCSL file suffix mf1), v2 is f (f1). The recordings are the same harp in both libraries: VSCO 2 CE's harp folder holds 23 files, 20 of them bit-identical to VCSL's mf1 files; VCSL adds an f layer for every zone, so VCSL is the source, with the two VSCO takes noted in problems.",
 "source": {
  "library": "VCSL (Versilian Community Sample Library), two takes from VSCO 2 Community Edition",
  "url": "https://github.com/sgossner/VCSL/tree/master/Chordophones/Composite%20Chordophones/Concert%20Harp",
  "alternateUrl": "https://github.com/sgossner/VSCO-2-CE/tree/master/Strings/Harp",
  "license": "CC0 1.0 (both repositories, LICENSE file: CC0 1.0 Universal)",
  "commit": {
   "vcsl": "c1ea7bcc3c73",
   "vsco": "440300901dfe"
  },
  "notes": "Folder 'Concert Harp' (files KSHarp_*), one round robin, layers mf and f (p or mp only at G1, D7 and F7). Not normalized: the levels are as played and recorded. Note names are scientific pitch (C4 = middle C), confirmed by measurement."
 },
